 "findings": [
  {
   "file": "plugins/specclaw/tests/run-infosec-evals.sh",
   "fp": "b05e92d68820",
   "in_delta": true,
   "line": 128,
   "message": "Any non-empty text from the model counts as an answered review, even with no `**Verdict:**` line. A refusal, an API error printed on stdout with exit 0, or truncated prose is scored as 'no finding'. On a fixed sample that lowers the false-alarm rate; on a vulnerable one it lowers the catch rate. The gate treats a report with no single valid verdict as 'did not run' (exit 3), but the runner only uses the gate's `--headings` output. Fix: treat a response with no valid verdict as no answer, at both call sites (vulnerable at ~120, fixed at ~128). Reuse the gate's verdict reading (for example a `--verdict` mode) rather than adding a second parser. Add rows for a stub that prints prose with no verdict, on both a vulnerable and a fixed sample.",
   "original_severity": "major",
   "pass": "review",
   "pre_existing": false,
   "rule": "B3",
   "scenario": "The stub `claude` prints `No findings.` (no `**Verdict:**` line), as the default stub branch and STUB_PROSE already do, for the dev-bypass fixed sample \u2192 f_answered=1, alarms=0 \u2192 `False-alarm rate: 0%` and exit 0. It should be UNKNOWN with a non-zero exit.",
   "severity": "major"
  },
  {
   "file": "plugins/specclaw/bin/specclaw-pr",
   "fp": "69e6b656d642",
   "in_delta": true,
   "line": 877,
   "message": "The block switch is honoured only for the exact string `true`. Any other spelling of a true value (`True`, `yes`, `on`, `\"true\"` if yaml_val keeps quotes) silently falls back to warn-only. That is the permissive default B6 and the base `Config` rule forbid for a security control. Fix: accept the YAML true spellings case-insensitively, and treat an unrecognised non-empty value as an error (die or flag) instead of 'advisory'. Add a table test over `True`, `yes` and `bogus`.",
   "original_severity": "major",
   "pass": "review",
   "pre_existing": false,
   "rule": "B6",
   "scenario": "config.yaml has `security_review_block: True` (valid YAML 1.1 boolean) and security-report.md has one unwaived `[BLOCK]` \u2192 specclaw-pr prints a warning, exits 0 and calls `gh pr create`. It should exit 1.",
   "severity": "major"
  },
  {
   "file": "plugins/specclaw/tests/run-infosec-evals.sh",
   "fp": "e0443ff2cb97",
   "in_delta": true,
   "line": 82,
   "message": "The comment says the eval passes 'the security rules a real run gives', but `rules_for` + `cat` feeds the whole preset and review-pack files: Layout, Rules, Tests, Shared and all non-security pack rules. Production (verify Step 3.52, build-context) passes only the `## Security` sections and the pack's security rules. The measured agent sees a different, larger prompt than the shipped one. Extract the `## Security` section the way specclaw-build-context does, or correct the comment.",
   "original_severity": "minor",
   "pass": "review",
   "pre_existing": false,
   "rule": "B4",
   "scenario": "",
   "severity": "minor"
  },
  {
   "file": "plugins/specclaw/tests/evals/infosec/dotnet-forwarded-rate-limit/fixed.cs",
   "fp": "841c7c94c330",
   "in_delta": true,
   "line": 10,
   "message": "Two 'fixed' samples break the rules shipped in this same diff. dotnet-forwarded-rate-limit/fixed.cs and python-forwarded-rate-limit/fixed.py key the login limiter on the client IP only. The dotnet and python `RateLimit` preset rules require keying 'on the account as well as the client' (node's xff fixed sample does key on email+IP). A rule-following reviewer may flag the fixed sample. That stays out of the BLOCK false-alarm count, but the 'fixed' baseline is not actually compliant. Key both on account+client.",
   "original_severity": "minor",
   "pass": "review",
   "pre_existing": false,
   "rule": "B1",
   "scenario": "",
   "severity": "minor"
  },
  {
   "file": "plugins/specclaw/bin/specclaw-security-gate",
   "fp": "5bc9b37fe086",
   "in_delta": true,
   "line": 54,
   "message": "Path and rule normalisation only handles the named shapes. `norm_path` strips a single trailing `:<digits>`, so `src/a.js:3-7` and `src/a.js:42:5` keep a suffix and a `src/a.js` waiver does not match. The rule token is only `bare()`d and then `rstrip`ped, so `` `B13`, `` or `(B13)` never equals `b13`. These fail closed, so a valid waiver silently does not lift the BLOCK. Strip a `:line[-line|:col]` suffix, and strip punctuation and backticks from both ends of the rule token. Add the rows to the path and rule tables.",
   "original_severity": "minor",
   "pass": "review",
   "pre_existing": false,
   "rule": "B1",
   "scenario": "",
   "severity": "minor"
  }
 ],
